\title{A Counterexample to ``Every Maximal-Fitness Limit Theory is Primitive and Rank-Minimal''}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 21, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study an evolutionary model of mathematical theories in which each theory $T$ carries a rational-valued \emph{fitness}
\[
f(T) = \frac{\mathrm{connections}(T)\cdot\mathrm{proofDensity}(T)}{\mathrm{axiomCount}(T)},
\]
together with a strict \emph{sub-theory order} $\sqsubset$ and a natural-number \emph{rank}. A natural conjecture asserts that \emph{every maximal-fitness limit theory is primitive (order-irreducible) and rank-minimal among maximal-fitness theories}, justified by two structural hypotheses: \emph{extension monotonicity} ($S \sqsubset T \Rightarrow f(S) \le f(T)$) and \emph{well-founded rank descent} ($S \sqsubset T \Rightarrow \mathrm{rank}(S) < \mathrm{rank}(T)$). We refute this conjecture via an explicit two-element model in which both hypotheses hold, a designated theory is simultaneously of maximal fitness, rank-minimal among maximal-fitness theories, and terminal, yet \textbf{fails to be primitive}. The refutation uses only finite case analysis and exact arithmetic over $\mathbb{Q}$, so it is non-circular and fully verifiable. We diagnose the failure as a conflation of \emph{local} terminality with \emph{global} order-minimality, and identify a precise repair via an anti-monotone \emph{parsimony} hypothesis.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A recurring program treats the corpus of mathematical theories as an evolving ecosystem: theories are born, extend one another, compete, and persist according to a fitness valuation. One attractive prediction is that the \emph{fittest} surviving theories should be the \emph{simplest}---irreducible foundations from which nothing smaller can be carved.

This paper isolates and tests a sharp form of that prediction. We call a theory \emph{primitive} when it has no proper sub-theory, \emph{terminal} when no fitness-increasing extension exists, and ask whether maximal fitness, together with two standard structural hypotheses, forces primitivity. We show it does not. The contribution is threefold: a minimal two-theory model demonstrating insufficiency; a structural diagnosis distinguishing local from global optimality; and a constructive repair via parsimony. All quantitative claims reduce to exact rational arithmetic, making the refutation immune to circularity.

\section{The model}

\begin{definition}[Theory landscape]
Let $\mathcal{T} = \{\mathsf{base}, \mathsf{ext}\}$ be the set of theories.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Proper sub-theory order]
The relation $\sqsubset$ on $\mathcal{T}$ is defined by $\mathsf{base} \sqsubset \mathsf{ext}$, and $S \sqsubset T$ is false for all other pairs. Thus $\mathsf{base}$ is the unique proper sub-theory of $\mathsf{ext}$, and $\mathsf{ext}$ has no proper extension.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rank]
$\mathrm{rank}: \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{N}$ is given by $\mathrm{rank}(\mathsf{base}) = 0$ and $\mathrm{rank}(\mathsf{ext}) = 1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Traits]
Each theory carries three rational traits:
\[
\mathrm{connections}(\mathsf{base}) = 1,\quad \mathrm{connections}(\mathsf{ext}) = 2,
\]
\[
\mathrm{proofDensity}(T) = 1 \ \forall T,\qquad \mathrm{axiomCount}(T) = 1 \ \forall T.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fitness]
The fitness of a theory is
\[
f(T) = \frac{\mathrm{connections}(T)\cdot\mathrm{proofDensity}(T)}{\mathrm{axiomCount}(T)} \in \mathbb{Q}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Primitive]
$T$ is \emph{primitive} iff there is no $S$ with $S \sqsubset T$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Maximal fitness]
$T$ has \emph{maximal fitness} iff $f(U) \le f(T)$ for every $U \in \mathcal{T}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rank-minimal among maximal-fitness theories]
$\mathrm{RankMinimalAmongMax}(T) :\Leftrightarrow \mathrm{MaxFitness}(T) \wedge (\forall U,\ \mathrm{MaxFitness}(U) \Rightarrow \mathrm{rank}(T) \le \mathrm{rank}(U))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Mutation and terminality]
A \emph{mutation} from $S$ to $T$ is a fitness-increasing proper extension: $\mathrm{Mutation}(S,T) :\Leftrightarrow S \sqsubset T \wedge f(S) < f(T)$. $T$ is \emph{terminal} iff it admits no outgoing mutation: $\mathrm{Terminal}(T) :\Leftrightarrow \lnot \exists U,\ \mathrm{Mutation}(T, U)$.
\end{definition}

The conjecture's proof strategy invokes two hypotheses, both holding in our model:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{(EM) Extension monotonicity:} $S \sqsubset T \Rightarrow f(S) \le f(T)$.
\item \textbf{(WF) Well-founded rank descent:} $S \sqsubset T \Rightarrow \mathrm{rank}(S) < \mathrm{rank}(T)$.
\end{itemize}

\section{The conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[refuted]
In any landscape satisfying \emph{(EM)} and \emph{(WF)}, every maximal-fitness limit theory is primitive and rank-minimal among maximal-fitness theories.
\end{conjecture}

The intended argument: by (EM) fitness never decreases as one extends; by (WF) the order is well-founded below; therefore the optimum should coincide with a primitive minimum. We show this reasoning is fallacious.

\section{Main results}

All proofs are by exhaustive case analysis over the two-element type and exact arithmetic over $\mathbb{Q}$.

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{fitness\_base}]
$f(\mathsf{base}) = 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Substitute the traits: $f(\mathsf{base}) = (1 \cdot 1)/1 = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{fitness\_ext}]
$f(\mathsf{ext}) = 2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Substitute the traits: $f(\mathsf{ext}) = (2 \cdot 1)/1 = 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{proper\_sub\_rank\_decreases}, WF]
For all $S, T$, if $S \sqsubset T$ then $\mathrm{rank}(S) < \mathrm{rank}(T)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The only case with $S \sqsubset T$ true is $(\mathsf{base}, \mathsf{ext})$, where $0 < 1$; all other cases discharge the hypothesis as false.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{extension\_monotone}, EM]
For all $S, T$, if $S \sqsubset T$ then $f(S) \le f(T)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Only $(\mathsf{base}, \mathsf{ext})$ has $S \sqsubset T$, and there $f(\mathsf{base}) = 1 \le 2 = f(\mathsf{ext})$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{base\_mutates\_to\_ext}]
$\mathrm{Mutation}(\mathsf{base}, \mathsf{ext})$ holds.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\mathsf{base} \sqsubset \mathsf{ext}$ by definition, and $f(\mathsf{base}) = 1 < 2 = f(\mathsf{ext})$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{ext\_maximal\_fitness}]
$\mathrm{MaxFitness}(\mathsf{ext})$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $U = \mathsf{base}$, $1 \le 2$; for $U = \mathsf{ext}$, $2 \le 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{ext\_rank\_minimal\_among\_max}]
$\mathrm{RankMinimalAmongMax}(\mathsf{ext})$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\mathsf{ext}$ is maximal-fitness. If $U = \mathsf{base}$ were maximal-fitness, then $f(\mathsf{ext}) \le f(\mathsf{base})$, i.e. $2 \le 1$, a contradiction; so the only maximal-fitness theory is $\mathsf{ext}$, with $\mathrm{rank}(\mathsf{ext}) \le \mathrm{rank}(\mathsf{ext})$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{ext\_terminal}]
$\mathrm{Terminal}(\mathsf{ext})$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A mutation $\mathrm{Mutation}(\mathsf{ext}, U)$ requires $\mathsf{ext} \sqsubset U$, but $\sqsubset$ has no edge out of $\mathsf{ext}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{ext\_not\_primitive}]
$\lnot\,\mathrm{Primitive}(\mathsf{ext})$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\mathsf{base} \sqsubset \mathsf{ext}$ witnesses a proper sub-theory.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{counterexample\_to\_maximal\_fitness\_primitive\_claim}]
There exists a theory $T$ such that
\[
\mathrm{MaxFitness}(T) \wedge \mathrm{RankMinimalAmongMax}(T) \wedge \mathrm{Terminal}(T) \wedge \lnot\,\mathrm{Primitive}(T).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $T = \mathsf{ext}$ and combine the preceding four lemmas.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Insufficiency]
Hypotheses \emph{(EM)} and \emph{(WF)} do not imply that maximal-fitness, rank-minimal, terminal theories are primitive. The conjecture is false.
\end{corollary}

\section{Structural diagnosis}

Two confusions drive the failure. First, \emph{local versus global optimality}: terminality is a local property ($T$ has no improving neighbor above it), whereas primitivity is global ($T$ has nothing below it). The witness $\mathsf{ext}$ is a local fitness maximum sitting atop the proper sub-theory $\mathsf{base}$. Second, the \emph{direction of the fitness--order coupling}: (EM) permits fitness to increase along $\sqsubset$, so the optimum migrates \emph{up} the order toward non-primitive elements; (WF) controls only chain length, not the optimum's location.

\section{A repair: parsimony-corrected optimality}

Replace (EM) by its converse:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{(PAR) Parsimony:} $S \sqsubset T \Rightarrow f(T) \le f(S)$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{proposition}[Repaired claim]
In a well-founded landscape satisfying \emph{(PAR)}, a maximal-fitness terminal theory is primitive and rank-minimal among maximal-fitness theories.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $T$ is maximal-fitness but not primitive, so some $S \sqsubset T$ exists. By (PAR), $f(T) \le f(S)$; by maximality $f(S) \le f(T)$; hence $f(S) = f(T)$ and $S$ is maximal-fitness. By (WF), $\mathrm{rank}(S) < \mathrm{rank}(T)$, so $T$ is not rank-minimal among maximal-fitness theories. Well-foundedness forces a primitive maximal-fitness element at the bottom of any descending chain.
\end{proof}

In the two-theory model, (PAR) would require $f(\mathsf{ext}) \le f(\mathsf{base})$---false as stated, which is precisely why the original model refutes the (EM)-based conjecture.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Algorithm A (Fitness evaluation).} Compute $f(T)$ in exact rational arithmetic; $O(1)$ per theory.

\textbf{Algorithm B (Predicate checking).} On a finite landscape of $n$ theories: maximal fitness in $O(n)$; terminality in $O(\deg T)$; primitivity in $O(n)$; rank-minimality among maxima in $O(n)$.

\textbf{Algorithm C (Counterexample search).} Enumerate theories; report any maximal-fitness, rank-minimal, terminal theory that is non-primitive. On the two-theory model this returns $\mathsf{ext}$ immediately. Total cost $O(n^2)$.

\section{Applications and discussion}

The result cautions against deriving global structural properties (irreducibility, minimality) from local optimality under a valuation---a pattern recurring in optimization, discrete search dynamics, and foundational modeling. Under an extension-monotone fitness, there is no reason to expect a ``simplest final theory'' at the base of mathematics; selection points toward whatever the landscape rewards.

\section{Future work}

Formalize the parsimony-corrected theorem in generality; abstract to arbitrary preorders with a linear-order valuation; study the $\mathrm{Mutation}$ dynamical system on larger landscapes; and quantify the separation (rank gap, chain length below an optimum) as a function of fitness increase along extensions.

\section{Conclusion}

A two-element model with elementary rational traits refutes the claim that maximal-fitness limit theories must be primitive and rank-minimal. Extension monotonicity and well-founded rank descent control monotonicity and chain length but not reducibility, because terminality is local while primitivity is global. Flipping a single inequality, via parsimony, restores the conclusion.

\end{document}

